 "check_text": "protocol.py: 2 biology problems (1 warning, 1 note).\n\n1. WARNING CONTAM-WELL: A used tip carried fwd 1 (tube A2 in slot 2), fwd 2 (tube A3 in slot\n2), rev 1 (tube B1 in slot 2) and rev 2 (tube B2 in slot 2) into 48 wells it was never meant\nfor\n   line 111:  p20.dispense(2, well_index_to_well(well_idx))\n   At line 111 the tip touched the liquid in wells C4, C5, C6, C7, C8, C9, C10, C11 and 40\n   more in slot 1 while it still carried fwd 1 (tube A2 in slot 2), fwd 2 (tube A3 in slot\n   2), rev 1 (tube B1 in slot 2) and rev 2 (tube B2 in slot 2) from an earlier step (this\n   line did it 48 times). A tip that has touched one liquid leaves a little of it in the\n   next liquid it touches. The protocol never transfers that liquid into these wells on\n   purpose, so it was not meant to be there. If the wells hold different samples, each now\n   holds a little of another.\n   Affected: wells C4, C5, C6, C7, C8, C9, C10, C11 and 40 more in slot 1.\n   Fix: Use a fresh tip for each well at line 111. If one tip must serve many wells,\n   dispense from above the liquid (for example `well.top()`) so that the tip never touches\n   what is already in the well.\n   Source: Lab Manager: minimizing cross-contamination in automated liquid handling\n           https://www.labmanager.com/minimizing-cross-contamination-in-automated-liquid-handling-workflows-35194\n   [d103c1286504]\n\n2. NOTE UNMIXED-AT-END: 72 wells never mixed after cdna 1, cdna 2, cdna 3, cdna 4 and 4 more\nwent in (10% of the well)\n   line 111:  p20.dispense(2, well_index_to_well(well_idx))\n   At line 111 the robot added cdna 1, cdna 2, cdna 3, cdna 4 and 4 more to wells A1, A2,\n   A3, A4, A5, A6, A7, A8 and 64 more in slot 1, which already held sybr, fwd 1, rev 1,\n   water and 4 more (this line did it 72 times). The addition was 10% of the well, nothing\n   mixed the well afterwards, and the run ended with them that way. Two liquids layered in a\n   small well do not mix on their own, so what the well holds is not at the concentration\n   you calculated. A PCR plate is usually vortexed and spun down before cycling; if this one\n   will not be, mix it.\n   Affected: wells A1, A2, A3, A4, A5, A6, A7, A8 and 64 more in slot 1.\n   Fix: Mix wells A1, A2, A3, A4, A5, A6, A7, A8 and 64 more in slot 1 after line 111:\n   `mix_after=(5, 16)` on the transfer, or `pipette.mix(5, 16, well)`, with the volume about\n   80% of what the well holds.\n   Source: Artel: automated liquid handlers as sources of error\n           https://www.bioprocessintl.com/assays/automated-liquid-handlers-as-sources-of-error\n   Source: Illumina: normalising library concentrations (pipette up and down 10 times to\n   mix)\n           https://support.illumina.com/ko-kr/bulletins/2017/03/best-practices-for-manually-normalizing-library-concentrations.html\n   Source: Thermo PowerTrack user guide (a reaction plate not mixed well gives variable\n   replicates)\n           https://documents.thermofisher.com/TFS-Assets/LSG/manuals/MAN0018825_PowerTrackSYBRGreenMasterMix_UG.pdf\n   Source: Integra: how to perform serial dilutions\n           https://www.integra-biosciences.com/global/en/applicationguide/master-art-pipetting-and-learn-how-perform-serial-dilutions-our-comprehensive-guide\n   [a767b1e78cd0]\n\nThe Opentrons simulator (8.8.2) passed this protocol with no error: it checks that the robot\ncan make the moves, not what they do to the liquids.",
